/**
\defgroup log1prel Relative logarithm
\brief Relative logarithm function \f$\psi(x) = \log(1 + x)/x\f$ and its derivatives in \f$x\f$ to order 6.

This module computes the relative logarithm \f$\psi(x) = \log(1 + x)/x\f$ and its derivatives \f$\psi^{(k)}(x)\f$ up to order \f$k = 6\f$. Include `<laplace/math/log1prel.hpp>` and call functions in namespace `laplace::math`, such as \ref laplace::math::log1prel "log1prel()" through \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d6 "log1prel_d6()". Every function is marked `LAPLACE_HD` for use on the host and inside Kokkos device kernels. Measured errors across all seven functions are at most 9.60 ulp on both gcc and CUDA, within test bounds up to 14.4 ulp. The name `log1prel` is Laplace's name, chosen by analogy with \ref exprel "exprel".

## Mathematical definition

For real argument \f$x > -1\f$ with \f$x \ne 0\f$, the relative logarithm \f$\psi(x)\f$ is defined by
\f[
\psi(x) = \frac{\log(1 + x)}{x} = \int_0^1 \frac{\mathrm{d}t}{1 + x t},
\f]
with \f$\psi(0) = 1\f$. Differentiating under the integral sign yields the \f$k\f$-th derivative
\f[
\psi^{(k)}(x) = \frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}x^k} \psi(x) = (-1)^k k! \int_0^1 \frac{t^k}{(1 + x t)^{k+1}} \, \mathrm{d}t.
\f]
Here \f$x \in (-1, \infty)\f$ is the real argument, \f$k \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ is the derivative order, and \f$t \in [0, 1]\f$ is the integration variable. For \f$k = 0\f$, the integral gives \f$\psi(x)\f$ itself, and at \f$x = 0\f$ it evaluates to
\f[
\psi^{(k)}(0) = (-1)^k \frac{k!}{k + 1}.
\f]

## Domain and special values

All seven functions share the open domain \f$x > -1\f$ and keep a constant sign across it. Because \f$1 + x t > 0\f$ for every \f$x > -1\f$ and \f$t \in [0, 1]\f$, the integrand \f$t^k (1 + x t)^{-(k+1)}\f$ is positive on \f$(0, 1]\f$. Consequently, \f$\psi(x) > 0\f$ everywhere on \f$(-1, \infty)\f$, and \f$\psi^{(k)}(x)\f$ has the constant sign \f$(-1)^k\f$ without zeros.

At \f$x = 0\f$, the functions return the exact values \f$\psi(0) = 1.0\f$ and \f$\psi^{(k)}(0) = (-1)^k k! / (k + 1)\f$. At the lower endpoint \f$x = -1\f$, \f$\psi(-1)\f$ returns \f$+\infty\f$, while \f$\psi^{(k)}(-1)\f$ returns \f$(-1)^k \infty\f$, meaning \f$-\infty\f$ for odd \f$k\f$ and \f$+\infty\f$ for even \f$k\f$. Every function returns \f$0.0\f$ as \f$x \to +\infty\f$. Inputs with \f$x < -1\f$ or NaN return quiet NaN.

## Method

For \f$x \ne 0\f$, the function value \f$\psi(x)\f$ is computed directly as `Kokkos::log1p(x) / x` (Trott and others, 2022). The derivatives \f$\psi^{(k)}(x)\f$ are evaluated across four regimes selected by \f$x\f$:

1. **Power series** for \f$|x| < 1/4\f$. Differentiating the Maclaurin series of \f$\psi(x)\f$ gives
   \f[
   \psi^{(k)}(x) = \sum_{m=0}^\infty \tau_m,
   \f]
   where \f$m \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}\f$ is the summation index. The initial term is \f$\tau_0 = (-1)^k k! / (k + 1)\f$, and consecutive terms follow the recurrence
   \f[
   \tau_{m+1} = -x \frac{(m + k + 1)^2}{(m + 1)(m + k + 2)} \tau_m.
   \f]
   At \f$x = 0\f$, this returns the exact value \f$\tau_0\f$.

2. **Gauss-Legendre quadrature** for \f$-4/5 < x \le 6\f$. The integral is evaluated with the 32-point Gauss-Legendre rule (Davis and Rabinowitz, 1984) on \f$[0, 1]\f$,
   \f[
   \psi^{(k)}(x) = (-1)^k k! \sum_{i=0}^{31} w_i \frac{t_i^k}{(1 + x t_i)^{k+1}},
   \f]
   where \f$t_i \in [0, 1]\f$ are the nodes and \f$w_i > 0\f$ are the weights for \f$i = 0, \dots, 31\f$. For \f$x > -4/5\f$, the pole at \f$t = -1/x\f$ lies strictly outside \f$[0, 1]\f$, and every term shares the same sign so no cancellation occurs.

3. **Split Gauss-Legendre quadrature** for \f$6 < x \le 20\f$. Because the integrand \f$t^k (1 + x t)^{-(k+1)}\f$ develops a sharp peak near \f$t = k / x\f$, a single 32-point rule on \f$[0, 1]\f$ fails to resolve it, and the forward recurrence cancels in this interval. Splitting \f$[0, 1]\f$ at \f$t = 1/4\f$ applies the 32-point rule on each of \f$[0, 1/4]\f$ and \f$[1/4, 1]\f$, using 64 nodes in total.

4. **Forward recurrence** for \f$x \le -4/5\f$ and \f$x > 20\f$. Differentiating \f$x \psi(x) = \log(1 + x)\f$ \f$k\f$ times and solving for \f$\psi^{(k)}(x)\f$ yields
   \f[
   \psi^{(k)}(x) = \frac{T_k - k \psi^{(k-1)}(x)}{x}, \qquad T_k = (-1)^{k-1} (k-1)! u^k, \qquad u = \frac{1}{1 + x},
   \f]
   started from \f$\psi^{(0)}(x) = \psi(x)\f$. This recurrence is numerically stable on these regions. For large \f$x\f$, however, the powers \f$u^k\f$ underflow and the resulting \f$\psi^{(k)}(x)\f$ fall below the normal double range, outside the promised accuracy.

## Accuracy

Error metrics, reference generation, backend evaluation, and test bounds are described on \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured". Because \f$\psi(x)\f$ and its derivatives \f$\psi^{(k)}(x)\f$ have no zeros on \f$(-1, \infty)\f$, the error measure is plain relative ulp. Reference evaluations cover finite normal double results from \f$1 + x = 2^{-53}\f$ to \f$x = 10^{300}\f$.

| Function | Order \f$k\f$ | Measured max error gcc (ulp) | Measured max error CUDA (ulp) | Test bound (ulp) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| \ref laplace::math::log1prel "log1prel()" | 0 | 1.28 | 1.28 | 2.0 |
| \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d1 "log1prel_d1()" | 1 | 3.94 | 3.94 | 6.0 |
| \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d2 "log1prel_d2()" | 2 | 5.03 | 5.03 | 7.6 |
| \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d3 "log1prel_d3()" | 3 | 6.39 | 6.39 | 9.6 |
| \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d4 "log1prel_d4()" | 4 | 7.06 | 7.06 | 10.6 |
| \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d5 "log1prel_d5()" | 5 | 8.27 | 8.27 | 12.4 |
| \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d6 "log1prel_d6()" | 6 | 9.60 | 9.60 | 14.4 |

See `docs/numerics.md` for the full measurement record.

## Speed

Evaluation speed depends on the regime and execution backend. On one gcc thread, calls take 20–60 ns in the power-series region (\f$|x| < 1/4\f$) and 20–37 ns for single quadrature (\f$-4/5 < x \le 6\f$). Calls take 59–68 ns for split quadrature (\f$6 < x \le 20\f$) and 22–50 ns for the forward recurrence (\f$x \le -4/5\f$ or \f$x > 20\f$). On CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER), \f$\psi(x)\f$ takes 0.46–0.63 ns per call. Derivative calls on CUDA take 2.8–3.7 ns with the series or single rule, 6.1–7.6 ns with the split rule, and 0.54–0.79 ns with the recurrence.

## Example

The following example evaluates \f$\psi(x)\f$ and its first two derivatives inside a `Kokkos::parallel_for` kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/math/log1prel.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void compute_log1prel(const Kokkos::View<const double*>& x,
                      Kokkos::View<double*>& val,
                      Kokkos::View<double*>& d1,
                      Kokkos::View<double*>& d2,
                      int n) {
  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_log1prel", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    const double x_val = x(i);
    val(i) = laplace::math::log1prel(x_val);
    d1(i)  = laplace::math::log1prel_d1(x_val);
    d2(i)  = laplace::math::log1prel_d2(x_val);
  });
}
\endcode

## Module functions

All seven functions in this module are called as `laplace::math::NAME`:

- \ref laplace::math::log1prel "log1prel()" — relative logarithm \f$\psi(x) = \log(1 + x)/x\f$
- \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d1 "log1prel_d1()" — first derivative \f$\psi'(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d2 "log1prel_d2()" — second derivative \f$\psi''(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d3 "log1prel_d3()" — third derivative \f$\psi^{(3)}(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d4 "log1prel_d4()" — fourth derivative \f$\psi^{(4)}(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d5 "log1prel_d5()" — fifth derivative \f$\psi^{(5)}(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::math::log1prel_d6 "log1prel_d6()" — sixth derivative \f$\psi^{(6)}(x)\f$

## References

- P. J. Davis and P. Rabinowitz (1984), Methods of Numerical Integration, 2nd edition, Academic Press. https://doi.org/10.1016/C2013-0-10566-1
- C. R. Trott and others (2022), Kokkos 3: Programming Model Extensions for the Exascale Era, IEEE Transactions on Parallel and Distributed Systems, 33(4), 805–817. https://doi.org/10.1109/tpds.2021.3097283
*/
